the controversial law in Texas banning  so-called sanctuary cities faces  resistant from law enforcement officials  across the state governor Greg Abbott  signed the measure known as SB 4 on  Sunday police and local officials could  face $25,000 fines or jail time if they  don't help enforce u.s. immigration law  David beg no spoke with the travis  county sheriff who was singled out by  the governor when he signed the law  David good morning Charlie good morning  the law goes into effect September 1st  let me tell you how it all got started  you have this new sheriff in Travis  County who came into office and said I  don't have the resources to enforce  federal immigration law all the time so  I'm not and the governor of Texas said  really you better or I'm going to take  away some state funding and we're going  to pass a law that could jail you or  remove you from office well the sheriff  didn't flinch and the governor made good  on his word I think it's bad for our  community in Austin Texas  Travis County Sheriff Sally Hernandez  says SB 4 hurts her deputies ability to  fight crime we have witnesses that are  undocumented that see a crime but  they're not going to feel comfortable  coming forward but governor Greg Abbott  says police cannot pick and choose which  laws they want to enforce he believes s  before we'll keep criminals off the  street this law cracks down policies  like the Travis County Sheriff who  declare that she would not detain known  criminals accused of violent crimes  misinformation based on fear I honor  detainers for the more violent crimes  like rape and murder right many Texas  sheriffs and police chiefs oppose the  new law saying they don't have the  resources to enforce federal immigration  law they also fear that SB 4 which  allows officers to ask crime victims or  witnesses their immigration status could  lead to racial profiling I think you  will see lawsuits from the different  organizations where individuals may feel  they're being targeted SP 4 has been  compared to Arizona's so-called show  your papers law which sparked protests  and boycotts in 2010 before it was  largely struck down by a federal court  and sheriff Hernandez hopes SB 4 dies  the same way if the law goes  to affect what are you going to do I  will have to change my policy because I  will not violate the law so the ACLU has  put out a travel advisory telling people  not to come to Texas because of the new  law  Norah yesterday the state of Texas filed  something of a preemptive strike the  Attorney General went to court and sued  the city of Austin he's trying to get a  judge to declare this new law  constitutional essentially trying to  shut down any further lawsuits against  the law quite a story David thank you so  much
 

